{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 1609,
    "dDurationMs": 4381,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The north pole is the top of the earth, and\nthe south pole is the bottom, of course..."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5990,
    "dDurationMs": 4939,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "except that the earth is kind of a ball, and\nthey don't really have tops and bottoms."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10929,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Granted, the earth isn't exactly spherical\nand it's spinning through space - spinning"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15549,
    "dDurationMs": 2901,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about an imaginary axis of rotation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 18450,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One of the points where that axis goes through\nthe earth, right here in the arctic ocean,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22350,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where the Russians planted their flag on the\nsea floor in 2007, is called the north pole..."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 26850,
    "dDurationMs": 2820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or rather, it's the \"geographic\" north pole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29670,
    "dDurationMs": 4090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because, just like there are different definitions\nfor what a \"year\" is, there are different"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 33760,
    "dDurationMs": 1270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "north poles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35030,
    "dDurationMs": 5049,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, compasses don't point to the\ngeographic, or spinning-top, north pole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40079,
    "dDurationMs": 4491,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They point to the MAGNETIC north pole, which\nis incidentally a magnetic south pole since"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44570,
    "dDurationMs": 1290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "opposites attract."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45860,
    "dDurationMs": 5750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The magnetic north (actually south) pole and\nsouth (actually north) pole are an electromagnet"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51610,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "caused by swirling convection currents in\nthe earth's liquid iron outer core."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56010,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These currents are heavily influenced by the\nrotation of the earth, so the magnetic field"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60170,
    "dDurationMs": 4769,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they generate roughly aligns with the earth's\naxis of rotation - but not precisely, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 64939,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not unchangingly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65939,
    "dDurationMs": 6031,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A hundred years ago, the magnetic north pole\nwas located in northern canada - over 2000km"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71970,
    "dDurationMs": 2210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "south of the geographic north pole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74180,
    "dDurationMs": 3860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it's been moving consistently north-west\nsince then, currently sitting in the middle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78040,
    "dDurationMs": 5810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the Arctic Ocean 450km south of the geographic\nnorth pole, and drifting about 55 kilometers"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 83850,
    "dDurationMs": 1699,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "closer to Russia each year."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85549,
    "dDurationMs": 4181,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What's more, the magnetic south pole is not\non the exact opposite side of the earth from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89730,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the magnetic north pole - it drifts around\nin a somewhat independent fashion and is currently"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94770,
    "dDurationMs": 3260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "20° closer to the equator than magnetic north!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98030,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Such is the nature of magnetic fields generated\nby confusingly swirly molten iron deep inside"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102850,
    "dDurationMs": 1040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103890,
    "dDurationMs": 4670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if you imagined, instead, that there were\njust a giant, perfect bar magnet inside the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108560,
    "dDurationMs": 4410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "earth, then that perfect bar magnet would\npoint towards the GEO-magnetic north pole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112970,
    "dDurationMs": 3700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which is currently located in Ellesmere Island,\nNunavut, Canada."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116670,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This pole, together with the geomagnetic south\npole which IS on the exact opposite side of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121450,
    "dDurationMs": 4570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the earth, represents the general, overall\ntrend of the earth's magnetic field, especially"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126020,
    "dDurationMs": 1700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as it extends into space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127720,
    "dDurationMs": 4290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So while it's not at all useful for compass\nnavigation on the earth's surface, it does"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132010,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "heavily influence the paths of solar wind\nparticles that cause the aurora - that is,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136170,
    "dDurationMs": 1429,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the northern lights."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 137599,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Beautiful."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138599,
    "dDurationMs": 5051,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In short, the north pole's whereabouts depend\non what you care about."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143650,
    "dDurationMs": 5111,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The earth spins like a top around the \"geographic\"\nnorth pole, compasses point to the \"magnetic\""
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 148761,
    "dDurationMs": 4249,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "north pole (which is actually a magnetic south\npole), and the northern lights are strongest"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 153010,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in a ring around the \"geomagnetic\" north pole\n(also a magnetic south pole)."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 157790,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All three north poles move, too - the magnetic\nand geomagnetic poles change quite drastically,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163750,
    "dDurationMs": 4430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but even the geographic north pole moves up\nto ten meters a year as the earth wobbles"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168180,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on its axis due to seasonal air pressure differences\nacross the globe, melting ice caps, and so"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 173120,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174120,
    "dDurationMs": 4199,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Assuming that the Russians planted their flag\nexactly at the geographic north pole in August"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178319,
    "dDurationMs": 6831,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "2007, not once since then has it been exactly\nat the pole - it was as far as 12 meters away"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185150,
    "dDurationMs": 6070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in 2009 and as close as 20 cm in 2010 - right\nnow it's about 3 and a half meters away."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191220,
    "dDurationMs": 5190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Basically, the earth is doing the biggest,\nslowest, pole dance ever around the Russian"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196410,
    "dDurationMs": 36170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "flag."
    } ]
  } ]
}
